pples gun. Cause I went from my six creed that was closer to seven pounds, like six, 13 or 14 to this one's like, it's six pounds, 0.1 ounces. It's like right there at six was quite a bit lighter. Um, I, I just don't have enough data yet to say, like, I don't think there's a right or wrong answer. Um, if you ask me right now, like, well, I don't even want to say, like, I was going to say, like, you're hunting a lot of elk, go with the six creed. If you're more deeter and occasional elk go with the 22 that that's just a me throwing out some random thing. Like I need more, more time with it. Um, it's a lighter gun, so I don't think I'm shooting it as accurately. Um, in my head going like with my six creed 600 yards all day long, shot that thing lights out this gun. I just hadn't, it was so busy this year. I, you know, it was the opposite of what we preach and like get out and practice and shoot a bunch. I mean, I, I barely got to shoot at all. Um, and most of that was a shorter distances. In fact, like the day we got in the field, it was like a big priority for me. Uh, the, the first day we were there where we couldn't hunt was like, Hey, I need to shoot some rocks at like 600, 700 yards and just get more confident and capable of this thing. Um, more confident is the right word. Um, but it's lighter, seems a little bit more finicky. I'm sorry. I've certainly like playing with that, uh, floating with like getting too light, you know? And that's like, but mounting on the tripod, add some weight and, uh, reduce, you know, so you're basically adding, cause you clamped to that tripod, you're adding weight to that whole system to help reduce recoil and keep it manageable. And, um, and, uh, yeah. Um, joy's still out. Maybe, maybe a year from now, I'm like, not 22 Creed's the bomb. Like this thing's fricking rad in that specific, um, setup that I got. I'm sure if I built the 22 Creed, uh, you know, added a pound to it and made it identical to my sixth Creed, you know, I, I don't know. They're both great. Um, right now I'm with you. Like if you just said gun to my head, pick one, I'd say six Creed, but that's just because I've got more experience with it. And I, and I haven't, I built this 22 lighter. I will say, um, it, a six pound gun and that mountain gear chassis, a 16 inch barrel, got the airlock nano on there. Uh, it's, I, it's, I packed a rifle around almost every day. I think there's one day I didn't when we, when we went and killed your and Justin's bears. Um, cause like, oh, I'm not hunting today. You guys are the hunters. We don't, you know, we're not gonna kill three animals today. Um, but packing that thing around, it's like, it's, it doesn't exist on the backpack anymore. Um, it's so compact and short and light that it's, it's almost like packing a toy around, you know, like you just don't, you don't have this big, heavy rifle that's sticking down below the pack or up above the pack. It's kind of non, you know, it's like going from packing like a big, heavy 90 millimeter spotter down the pack and a 55. Like it's just like two completely different things. Um, and not, not to say that my six creed is heavy or big or bulky. It wasn't. Um, but if you'd like compare like a traditional rifle to now put into super lightweight folding chassis, it's, um, it's pretty fricking rad. I'll say that. I made me think this is a tangent, but the rifle chassis. And we have a video we've shown like how to carry a rifle chassis and generally have to use a locking buckle to keep that secure. But those new K4X buckles, non locking, but the way that they bite, um, like I knew that they were better in terms of how they hold tension, but it completely reinforced it on this hunt. Just how phenomenal those things are holding and not being a, an actively locking buckle. Yeah, no, it's, uh, it's two things. The existing K4 YKK buckle we use, the standard one is, is quite frankly, terrible. Um, that's why we have to have the, you have to have a locking buckle for it. There's just so much tolerance built into how the webbing weaves through there. Um, that, uh, they just don't bite well when it's not pulling, um, perfectly linear. Um, it doesn't bite very well. So you kind of have to use a locking buckle. And then obviously the K4X ones, it's one of the reasons I wanted to design custom buckles. And that was, you know, like I was like top three on the list of like, here's the features I want. And that's for the webbing to bite. So you, you pull that sucker tight, um, and it's not going to slip. Not, not that it won't, it will slip at a certain angle. I believe it's 38 degrees, um, in our testing that we did. Uh, but that's like compared to like 15 degrees of the YKK buckle. Um, so yeah, they work awesome, which was good. That was a lot of work to put into it and to have the result be like, all right, this is what, what we set out to achieve. All right. This next question came through from Lauren. He said, I'm getting ready to leave for my first solo back country hunt, uh, for context, he's in Washington. He's doing a hunt there. He said it wasn't supposed to be solo, but everyone backed out. But at this point I'm committed to going. He said, I've been planning for this hunt for a year, training for it and slowly buying all new gear from pack to boots to rifle to basically everything. He said, but now that I'm looking at going completely, completely second. Sorry. Now that I'm looking at completely going solo, it has me second guessing a few things. My main question is what changes in your pack when you're hunting solo versus with a buddy? Um, he mentioned things like his first aid kit's pretty minimal. Does he need to carry more? Does he need to carry other emergency gear? Does he need to carry more food than it normally would? Does he leave a spotter behind to save weight considering the potential of a solo pack out? And he basically summarizes and says, where do you add weight because you're alone? And where do you cut weight because there's nobody to split gear with? So what changes? Yeah. Um, nothing for me. Um, Oh, look, it's such a great question. The answer is so boring. No, I mean, you go back 15, 20 years ago, gear was heavier. Uh, absolutely. When I'm hunting with a partner, we're packing, I guess nothing's not correct. Cause like on this hunt, you packed a bipod, I packed a tripod for rifle support. Um, I think that's the only difference though. Uh. Yeah. I mean, your average hunt that you and I go on in Idaho, like call it, cause he's in Washington. So we're not talking Alaska. We're not talking expedition. We're not talking like remote fly and stuff. We're just keeping this like lower 48 year backpack hunting. I do think the answer is nothing. Yeah. Yeah. You said 15, 20 years ago, gear was heavier. We absolutely, like we would pack a stove. Um, we would, I don't know. There's not a whole lot you, you know, tripod optics, stuff like that. Um, the, I guess, I mean, as I'm saying this, as I say, nothing like we do spine scopes one, like we just packed a spine scope on this trip. Um, so that was like Tyler carried that most of the time. I think I carried it for the first day or something, but, um, if you're solo, you are now responsible for everything. Um, but generally our gear is so light, so dialed in. And I really just like being entirely self-sufficient. So if you ever got in a situation where, you know, you end up from your hunting partner, um, you guys go different directions to go glass or whatever. And you're like, oh, we'll meet back up here tonight. If that doesn't happen, I like the idea of being entirely self-sufficient. We had this, um, on the, on the, in Alaska here, um, completely different example, but with our pack rafts, um, I, everybody, I made sure everybody in their raft had a repair kit and a pump. Um, so that if you were, you know, there's four of us, four rafts. If you're the fourth raft, the other three go around a bend in the river. All of a sudden you drag across some crazy sharp rock and everyone ends up two miles downstream before someone looks over their shoulder and goes, well, where's Steve at? Um, I had the ability to pair, repair my raft and blow it back up. Um, seems like a simple thing. Like, oh, we just, we got a repair kit and a pump. We're good. We don't need four of them. But I made sure we had redundancy there. Um, especially when you're talking Alaska and the risks of that. Um, but I like that, you know, apply that concept to a back country hunt, like being self-sufficient is a good thing. So at the very minimal, if you're hunting with a buddy, um, you know, if you get separated, just make sure you got everything to, to be safe for a couple of days. You know, the, the stove and your sleeping bag and pad and a tent and whatnot. If your buddy's got the tent and you don't, um, then obviously you like, you just don't split up during the day. But I, I, I like the idea of having that, um, independence, um, to where you could be alone and be just fine. Um, but yeah, at this, at this point it would just be spine scope and tripod would be the two things. Like if you and I are hunting together or hunting a group, it's like, all right, we only need one spotter. We can share a tripod, um, depending on the hunt and the type of glass that we're going to be doing. And then there is some aspect of like, okay, are we, you know, frankly for me, if it's like, oh, we're only going three to five miles in, I'm going to pack a few extra pounds of stuff. I don't care anymore. Um, if we're doing like, oh, we're going 20 miles in then, yeah, then you start counting ounces and okay, let's, let's only pack one spotter. Let's only pack one tripod. Um, you start saving some weight. All right, let's hit this one to wrap up for today. It's a, I think another great question. Um, Jeremy wrote in and said, it seems like nowadays for archery elk, people don't say you have the time to range because everything happens so fast. But sometimes I feel like guys make more bad shots because they're misjudging distance. He said, I'm of the opinion that I would rather lose an opportunity because I couldn't get an exact range than just guess a range and hope I get the distance right when I take a shot. He says, I feel like I remember you guys touching on this topic at some point, but I can't find the podcast. What are your guys thoughts on this? Yeah. Yeah. We talked about that some part of this year. I think, um, I'm absolutely of his opinion, uh, unless it's just slam dunk. Like I know it's 30 yards and under, you know, it's like, it's just, it's dead. Uh, if it's, um, maybe I answered this slightly different, but I would say like, once I kind of go past 30 yards, I'm going to like a hundred percent, I'm ranging every single time. And I think if you look, you know, it's very, very easy to get caught in the moment of that shot. Like very, like, oh my gosh, animals there. I need to shoot now. Uh, I would say from experience and you could probably ask a thousand hunters this. And if they're honest, they probably like you, you always have more time than you think. You just put the pressure on. I need to shoot now. Um, and you got to get out of that mindset. Cause that is what creates errors, right? Like you're just, you're not only not ranging, but you're then rushing the shot, like draw anchor anchor, shoot, you know, like real, everything's happening so fast. It happens in a blur. You need to like take that extra. It's like a half a second, man, to like slow down, process everything. Um, obviously ranging takes longer than half a second, but it's just like a little bit of, uh, put the brakes on, assess everything, make sure you're good. And you, you always have that extra second or two to make a good shot. Um, so yeah, past 30 yards I'm ranging. And then, oh yeah, where I was going with this is in an individual hunt, I could certainly see, you could argue like, oh, I didn't have time to range. I just drew back real quick, shot, killed the animal. Um, and you probably killed it because you didn't, didn't range that you, you know, you truly didn't have the time. I just think that's the exception, not the rule. Um, so if you were to take that scenario or, you know, elk's coming in and you choose to range or not range. I think if you range, if you had, if you could repeat that scenario a hundred times, if you choose to range and then shoot, I guarantee you, um, your success rate is higher over that hundred shots sequences, uh, situations than it is if you chose never to range in that hundred shots and just winged a shot as fast as you can. So no, it costs you an individual animal. But I think if you look at the sum of a bunch of experiences, this goes back to all the small caliber stuff, right? Like maybe there's a situation which a 300 short mag punches through that super thick bone and a six creed doesn't, but over a larger sample size, uh, you're going to have more consistent results if you take the time to range. Yeah. And it just going back, it just slow, like slows that process down, forces you to think, forces you to assess, forces you to like, oh, where am I actually aiming? Um, cause I was super guilty of this, like early twenties. Um, you know, you just, you finally get that elk in, like you've been hunting hard for three, four days and all, it's like all this adrenaline pressure, you got buck fever, whatever. Um, and you just rush a shot. And I think that's going to cost you way, way, way, way more times than it is to stop and range and then shoot. Um, and there's little things, um, what's his name? Alan Bolin. Um, he's, uh, I just see him on Instagram. Um, uh, he was, uh, I finished met him. Uh, I did a podcast with Hoyt and they have like different hosts and he was the hosts. Um, but he, he, I saw a video on Instagram the other day where he has his range finder in a pouch and it's on a tether, um, off to the side. And so he could range and he's 